🏥 Federated Graph Neural Networks for Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD) Prediction
📌 Project Overview

This project focuses on Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD) prediction using Federated Learning (FL) combined with Graph Neural Networks (GNNs).
To preserve data privacy across institutions, multiple hospital datasets are trained collaboratively without sharing raw patient data. Each hospital constructs its own patient graph, and model training is performed in a federated, non-IID setting.

🛠️ Setup & Tools

The following tools and technologies were used:

Programming Language: Python

IDE: Visual Studio Code (VS Code)

Version Control: Git & GitHub

Libraries & Frameworks:

NumPy, Pandas

Scikit-learn

PyTorch

PyTorch Geometric

SciPy (for ARFF handling)

The project folder is structured to clearly separate datasets, preprocessing scripts, graph construction, and federated training modules.

📊 Dataset Collection

UCI Machine Learning Repository: CKD dataset (ARFF format)

Kaggle: CKD-related datasets (CSV format)

Dataset documentation and feature descriptions were reviewed to ensure:

Clinical relevance

Compatibility with federated learning

Feasibility of feature alignment across datasets

🧹 Data Cleaning

Converted UCI CKD dataset from ARFF to CSV

Handled missing values using appropriate imputation strategies

Removed inconsistencies and invalid records

Standardized column naming conventions

Cleaned datasets were saved as intermediate CSV files for reproducibility

⚙️ Feature Preprocessing

Identified and matched common clinical features across datasets

Imputed missing values (numerical & categorical)

Encoded class labels for CKD prediction

Normalized numerical features

Saved final cleaned and preprocessed CSV files

🏥 Hospital Simulation (Federated, Non-IID Setting)

To simulate a real-world healthcare scenario:

Hospital-A: UCI CKD dataset

Hospital-B & Hospital-C: Kaggle CKD datasets

Each hospital:

Maintains local data only

Exhibits non-IID label distribution (realistic label skew)

Performs its own train–validation split

This setup closely mimics federated learning across independent medical institutions.

🕸️ Graph Construction (GNN-Ready)

Nodes: Individual patients

Edges: Constructed using K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) based on clinical similarity

Node Features: Preprocessed clinical attributes

Labels: CKD classification

Graphs were created using PyTorch Geometric, ensuring:

Compatibility with GNN models

Readiness for federated training across hospitals

Each hospital maintains its own graph, enabling privacy-preserving federated GNN training.

🚀 Key Highlights

Privacy-preserving federated learning

Realistic non-IID hospital data simulation

Graph-based patient modeling

End-to-end pipeline: raw data → federated GNN-ready graphs

